+/// # The optional cap
+///
+/// A face that jumps an hour between two frames is as jarring as one that goes
+/// back, and for a clock someone is *watching* the correction is better taken
+/// as a run of large steps than as one. [`Session::with_max_advance`] bounds how
+/// far a single reading may move past the previous one:
+///
+/// ```text
+/// reading = min(max(wall_now, anchor + monotonic_elapsed, last), last + cap)
+/// ```
+///
+/// The reading then closes on the wall clock at `cap` per reading and lands on
+/// it, so the smoothing is finite and the clock ends up where the system says.
+/// Monotonicity is untouched — the cap only lowers a value that was already
+/// `>= last`.
+///
+/// **The cap must exceed the gap between readings**, or the reading falls behind
+/// the wall clock for good: at one reading a second and a cap of half a second
+/// the clock runs at half speed. `ucal wallclock` refuses caps under
+/// [`MIN_MAX_ADVANCE_NANOS`] for that reason. With no cap, which is the default,
+/// nothing above changes.
+///
 /// # What it does not do
